 two different tribes  start with song a ceremonial dance  and challenge demanding that the other  chief bows and pays respect  and the queens they're now helping to  lead the tribe  we say things now and we feel things now  that relate to  the culture and to the neighborhood and  and to the other girls that see us  not as just  a  complimentary piece  but as  a symbol  to look up to and something to be  the tradition of gratitude dates back to  the mid-19th century  when runaway slaves found refuge with  native american tribes who themselves  suffered persecution that's saying thank  you for helping us  and it's done with pride dr maurice  martinez has chronicled that history for  more than 50 years in both his book and  documentary black indians of new orleans  carnival by itself the meaning of  carnival day topsy-turvy day  anyone could be anything  we're gonna meet everybody  they wanted to maintain their dignity as  human beings i'm a man  i'm the big chief  i won't bow down on that dirty ground in  the days of segregation and even after  masking allowed an ability to walk with  pride when many new orleanians face the  humiliation of racism did you ever  expect to be in here no i never my  wildest dream i never this mardi gras  season mardi gras indian culture was on  full display at the historic new orleans  collection we had to betray the native  american to be ourself because here in  the south everything about african was  forbidden couldn't talk about it  throughout its exhibit making mardi gras  the museum hoped to rectify decades of  discrimination and neglect  rex king of carnival one of the earliest  parade organizations once discriminated  against blacks jews italians and irish i  think it's a big note of equality that  we have a mardi gras indian suit next to  rex lydia blackmore is the decorative  arts curator it's hard to learn about  mardi gras indians for a long long time  they didn't want you to learn about what  they're doing it just wasn't the